    "query": "What does the Bhagavad Gita say about finding inner peace?",
    "response": "**\ud83d\udcd6 Bhagavad Gita Teaching on Finding Inner Peace**\n\n**\ud83d\udd49\ufe0f Sanskrit Verse (BG 2.70):**\n*\u0906\u092a\u0942\u0930\u094d\u092f\u092e\u093e\u0923\u092e\u091a\u0932\u092a\u094d\u0930\u0924\u093f\u0937\u094d\u0920\u0902 \u0938\u092e\u0941\u0926\u094d\u0930\u092e\u093e\u092a\u0903 \u092a\u094d\u0930\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0928\u094d\u0924\u093f \u092f\u0926\u094d\u0935\u0924\u094d*\n\n**\ud83c\udf1f Translation:**\n\"As waters enter the ocean which is ever being filled but is always still, so one who is not disturbed by desires can alone achieve peace\"\n\n**\ud83d\udcda Original Context:**\nThe metaphor of the ocean for a peaceful mind. This teaching came when Arjuna was facing a crisis of dharma on the battlefield of Kurukshetra.\n\n**\ud83c\udfaf Modern Application:**\n\n**The Timeless Wisdom:**\nWhat Krishna taught Arjuna 5,000 years ago applies directly to your situation today. Just as Arjuna was paralyzed by doubt and conflicting duties, we too face moments where we don't know the right path.\n\n**Real-Life Example:**\nConsider a modern professional facing finding inner peace - perhaps a doctor who must choose between a high-paying private practice and serving in rural areas. The Gita's guidance remains the same: understand your svadharma (true nature), act according to dharma (righteousness), and surrender the results to the divine.\n\n**Practical Steps:**\n1. **Self-Inquiry:** Meditate on your true nature and natural inclinations\n2. **Dharmic Action:** Choose the path that serves the greater good\n3. **Detached Engagement:** Perform your duty without attachment to results\n4. **Divine Surrender:** Offer all actions as worship to the Supreme\n\n**\ud83d\udd2e Krishna's Promise:**\nIn verse 9.22, Krishna assures: \"To those who are constantly devoted and who always remember Me with love, I give the understanding by which they can come to Me.\"\n\n**\ud83d\udcab Integration Practice:**\nChant this verse daily and contemplate its meaning. Let the ancient wisdom guide your modern decisions.\n\n---\n*Reference: Bhagavad Gita BG 2.70 - Authentic translation from traditional commentaries*"
